<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Tigray</h1><p class="st-meta">1748 CE – 1855 CE (approx.) · capital Adwa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern Ethiopian province whose lords dominated the empire during the Era of the Princes, from Ras Mikael Sehul to Dejazmach Wube.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the mid-18th century, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> emperors lost power, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul">Mikael Sehul</a> built up a strong regional state in Tigray, the old heartland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, controlling the trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>. By 1748 he was effectively ruler of the province, and in 1769 he had Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyoas-i">Iyoas</a> killed and made himself master of the court.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tigray remained formally part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> but its lords acted as independent princes, with seats at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalo">Antalo</a>. They profited from firearms imported through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolde-selassie">Ras Wolde Selassie</a>, who received the British envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-salt">Henry Salt</a>, and Dejazmach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabagadis-woldu">Sabagadis</a> continued the tradition of Tigrayan power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1855 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Kassa Hailu</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wube-haile-maryam">Wube Haile Maryam</a>, lord of Tigray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semien">Semien</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-deresge">Battle of Deresge</a> and was crowned Tewodros II, ending the Era of Princes. Tigray remained a powerful province, and its lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Kassa Mercha</a> later became Yohannes IV.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tigray.jpg" alt="Tigray" width="320" height="183"><figcaption>Image: Flag of the Tigray Region; Image made by J. Ollé also see es:Wikipedia:Autorizaciones/Banderas de Jaume Ollé · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tigray_Region.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1748 CE – 1855 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Adwa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mikael Sehul, Wolde Selassie, Sabagadis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tigrinya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ethiopia, Eritrea</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Tigray, Tigre</dd></dl></aside></div>

